<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

    <worksheet margin="1in" bottom="1.25cm" xml:id="vector-spaces-complete">
    <title>Vector Spaces (Complete)</title>
        <page>
        
            <exercise workspace="2in">
            <title>Motivation</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        Wave functions live in a more general mathematical space which has a useful structure.
                        We can draw intuition from how we've previously defined <q>vector</q>, but it is important
                        to understand <q>vectors</q> in a more general sense.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>

            <exercise workspace="6in">
            <title>Vector Space</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                A <term>vector space</term> is a set <m>V</m> (called <term>vectors</term>) over <m>\R</m> or <m>\C</m> (the <term>scalars</term>)
                such that
                
                    </p>
                </introduction>
                <task workspace="1in">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            <term>Addition</term>
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            of vectors is defined. That is, we know how to take any two vectors in <m>V</m> and produce
                            another vector in <m>V</m>.
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </task>
                <task workspace="1in">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            <term>Scalar</term>
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            multiplication is defined. That is, we know how to multiply and vector from <m>V</m> by a scalar
                            to produce another vector in <m>V</m>.
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </task>
                <task workspace="1in">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            These operations behave <q>nicely</q>:
                            <ol>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>\uvec + \vvec = \vvec + \uvec</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>(\uvec + \vvec)+\wvec = \uvec + (\vvec + \wvec)</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        There exists a vector <m>\mathbf{0}</m> such that <m>\mathbf{0}+ \vvec = \vvec</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                       For every vector <m>\vvec</m>, there is another vector <m>\uvec</m> which is 
                                       an additive inverse: <m>\vvec + \uvec = \mathbf{0}</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                       For every vector <m>\vvec</m>, <m>1 \cdot \vvec = \vvec</m> 
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>a(b\vvec ) = (ab)\vvec</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>a(\uvec + \vvec) = a\uvec + a\vvec</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>(a+b)\vvec= a\vvec + b\vvec</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                            </ol>
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            Note that in quantum mechanics, you typically work in a <term>complex vector space</term> (a vector 
                            space whose scalars are complex numbers).
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </task>
            </exercise>
</page>
<page>
            <exercise workspace="3in">
                <title>Examples: Vector Spaces </title>
             
                <statement>
                    <p>
                       
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
            
                    
  
  <tabular halign="center">
    <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
      <cell><m>V</m></cell>
      <cell>Scalar</cell>

    </row>
    <row>
      <cell><m>\R^2</m></cell>
      <cell><m>\R</m></cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell><m>\R^3</m></cell>
      <cell><m>\R</m></cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell><m>\R^n</m></cell>
      <cell><m>\R</m></cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell><m>\C^n</m></cell>
      <cell><m>\C</m></cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell><m>\mathbb{P}^n</m> (polynomials of degree <m>\leq n</m>)</cell>
      <cell><m>\R</m></cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell><m>C(X)</m> (continuous functions from <m>X \to \R</m>)</cell>
      <cell><m>\R</m></cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>Linear combinations of functions of complex-valued functions <m>\{\psi_n\}_{n=1}^\infty</m></cell>
      <cell><m>\C</m></cell>
    </row>
  </tabular>
<p>
           The last three examples are known as <term>function spaces</term> because the vectors are functions.
        </p>
        <p>
            Note that the last example is a typical vector space in quantum mechanics, where the <m>\psi_n</m> functions are
            solutions to Schrödinger's equation.
        </p>
                    
                </solution>
          
            </exercise>
</page>
<page>
        <exercise workspace="8in">
        <title>Vector Magnitude</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
           The definition of a vector space has no requirement about defining the magnitude of a vector. This is additional
           structure that must be defined, and that we can do in a natural way:
            </p>
           
        </statement>
        
        <solution>
            <p>
                Recall that in <m>\R^n</m>, the dot product is used to compute magnitude:
                <md>
                    |\vvec| = \sqrt{\vvec \cdot \vvec}
                </md>
                This motivates a general way to define magnitude of a vector in any vector space <m>V</m>:
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Define a valid <term>inner product</term> of vectors (a way to combine any two vectors 
                            to produce a scalar).
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Use the inner product to define magnitude as <m>|\vvec| := \sqrt{\langle \vvec, \vvec\rangle}</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
                
            </p>
        </solution>
        </exercise>
</page>
<page>

        <exercise workspace="5in">
        <title>More on Inner Products</title>
       <statement>
        <p>
           There are some necessary properties an inner product must satisfy to make it a valid 
           inner product. For example, we must have that <m>\vvec \cdot \vvec \geq 0 </m>. But what makes a valid 
           definition a <q>good</q> definition?
        </p>
       </statement> 
       <solution>
        <p>
            Recall that with the dot product in <m>\R^2</m>, <m>\vvec \cdot \wvec =0</m> when <m>\vvec</m> and 
            <m>\wvec</m> are perpendicular (orthogonal). In the sense of projections, such vectors are as dissimilar (indpendent)
            as they could be. 
        </p>
        <p>
            This is our intuition: for any vector space <m>V</m>, <m>\langle \vvec, \wvec \rangle = 0</m> should imply a notion
            of independence between <m>\vvec</m> and <m>\wvec</m>.
        </p>
       </solution>
       
        </exercise>
    
    </page>


       
    </worksheet>
